+# Contract: `accounts` command-line interface
+
+**Feature**: `001-accounts-cli` | **Spec**: [../spec.md](../spec.md)
+
+This is the tool's only external interface. Every statement here is assertable, and each row
+names the requirement it comes from. Message strings are exact and complete: no prefix, no
+suffix, no trailing punctuation beyond the single terminating newline.
+
+---
+
+## Synopsis
+
+```text
+python -m accounts --store PATH COMMAND USERNAME
+```
+
+- `COMMAND` is one of `register`, `login`, `unlock`, `passwd`, `delete`. (FR-001)
+- `PATH` is the single store file the invocation acts on. It is created on first successful
+  write and reused by later invocations. (FR-001, FR-002)
+- `USERNAME` identifies the account. (FR-001)
+- `src/` must be on `PYTHONPATH` for `python -m accounts` to resolve. (spec Assumptions)
+- Requires Python 3.11 or newer; standard library only. (spec Assumptions; Principle I)
+
+### Argument grammar
+
+| Input | Behaviour |
+|---|---|
+| `--store PATH COMMAND USERNAME` | The documented form. |
+| `--store` appearing after the positionals | Accepted — the flag is located by scan, not by position. (D-002) |
+| `--store=PATH` | **Usage error.** Only the space-separated form is supported. (D-002) |
+| `--store` with no following value | Usage error. |
+| `--store` omitted | Usage error. (FR-006) |
+| `COMMAND` missing, or not one of the five | Usage error. (FR-006) |
+| `USERNAME` missing | Usage error. (FR-006) |
+| A third positional, or any other flag (including `-h`/`--help`) | Usage error. |
+
+**No password is ever an argument.** The accepted arguments are the store path, the command
+word and the username only; a password supplied where an argument is expected is never read
+as a password and yields a usage error. (FR-003, SC-007)
+
+### Usage error
+
+```text
+usage: python -m accounts --store PATH {register|login|unlock|passwd|delete} USERNAME
+```
+
+on **stderr**, exit **2**, and no store file created or modified. (FR-006)
+
+---
+
+## Standard input protocol
+
+Each password is one line of standard input: the content up to and excluding the line break,
+or up to end of input. (FR-003)
+
+| Command | Lines read, in order |
+|---|---|
+| `register` | 1 — the new password |
+| `login` | 1 — the password |
+| `unlock` | 0 — reads nothing |
+| `passwd` | 2 — the current password, then the new password |
+| `delete` | 1 — the current password |
+
+- Input beyond those lines is **ignored**, not an error. (FR-004)
+- Standard input is decoded as UTF-8; `\r\n` and `\n` both terminate a line. (D-003)
+- Leading and trailing whitespace inside a line is **preserved**; a password may contain
+  spaces. Only the line break is removed. (FR-003)
+- An **empty line** is a password of length 0 → `password too short` (not missing input).
+- **End of input before a required line** → `missing password input` on stderr, exit **2**,
+  store unchanged. (FR-005)
+
+---
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+| Status | Meaning | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| 0 | Success | FR-030 |
+| 1 | Invalid credentials | FR-030 |
+| 2 | Invalid input — username format, password length, usage, or missing input | FR-030 |
+| 3 | Account conflict — already exists, or does not exist | FR-030 |
+| 4 | Account locked | FR-030 |
+| 5 | Store unusable | FR-030 |
+
+Every successful command exits 0 and writes **nothing to stderr**. Every failing command
+writes its message to **stderr** and nothing to stdout. (FR-027)
+
+---
+
+## Messages
+
+| Message | Stream | Status | Produced by |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `registered USERNAME` | stdout | 0 | `register` success (FR-009) |
+| `ok` | stdout | 0 | `login` success (FR-012) |
+| `invalid credentials` | stderr | 1 | `login` wrong password or unknown account; `passwd`/`delete` wrong password (FR-013, FR-014, FR-020, FR-024) |
+| `invalid username` | stderr | 2 | `register` with a username failing FR-007 (FR-010) |
+| `password too short` | stderr | 2 | `register`/`passwd` with a password under 12 characters (FR-010, FR-021) |
+| `missing password input` | stderr | 2 | end of input before a required password line (FR-005) |
+| `usage: python -m accounts --store PATH {register\|login\|unlock\|passwd\|delete} USERNAME` | stderr | 2 | malformed arguments (FR-006) |
+| `username already exists` | stderr | 3 | `register` of an existing username (FR-011) |
+| `no such account` | stderr | 3 | `unlock`/`passwd`/`delete` on an unknown account (FR-018, FR-022, FR-025) |
+| `account locked` | stderr | 4 | `login` on a locked account, correct password or not (FR-016) |
+| `store unusable: <cause>` | stderr | 5 | unreadable, unwritable or corrupt store (FR-029) |
+
+`unlock`, `passwd` and `delete` print **nothing at all** on success — exit 0 with both streams
+empty. (FR-017, FR-019, FR-023; FR-027)
+
+`store unusable: ` is a fixed prefix; what follows names the specific cause and is not pinned
+to an exact string. Tests assert the prefix (SC-002) and that the cause is non-empty
+(Principle II). (FR-029)
+
+---
+
+## Per-command behaviour
+
+Checks are applied in the order listed; the first one that fails determines the outcome.
+Global order for every command: argument parsing (2) → store load (5) → read required input
+lines (2) → the command's own checks below. (D-004)
+
+### `register USERNAME`
+
+1. Username fails FR-007 → `invalid username`, stderr, **2**, no account created.
+2. Password under 12 characters → `password too short`, stderr, **2**, no account created.
+3. Username already in the store → `username already exists`, stderr, **3**; the existing
+   account's password, tally and lock state are unchanged.
+4. Otherwise → create the account (fresh salt, `failed_logins: 0`, `locked: false`), print
+   `registered USERNAME` on stdout, exit **0**, store written.
+
+Username is checked **before** the password, and both format rules **before** the duplicate
+check: an invalid username with a short password prints `invalid username` and exits 2; an
+existing username with a short password prints `password too short` and exits 2. (FR-010)
+
+### `login USERNAME`
+
+1. No such account → `invalid credentials`, stderr, **1** — indistinguishable in output and
+   status from an existing account with a wrong password. No store write. (FR-014)
+2. Account locked → `account locked`, stderr, **4**. Nothing changes: not the tally, not the
+   lock, not the password. `ok` is not printed. (FR-016)
+3. Password incorrect → `invalid credentials`, stderr, **1**, tally +1; if the tally reaches 5
+   the account becomes locked, and that fifth attempt is still reported as
+   `invalid credentials` / 1. (FR-013, FR-015)
+4. Otherwise → `ok` on stdout, exit **0**, tally reset to 0. (FR-012)
+
+### `unlock USERNAME`
+
+1. Reads no input. (FR-017)
+2. No such account → `no such account`, stderr, **3**. (FR-018)
+3. Otherwise → clear `locked`, reset the tally to 0, exit **0**, no output. Succeeds whether
+   or not the account was locked. (FR-017)
+
+### `passwd USERNAME`
+
+Reads two lines: current password, then new password.
+
+1. No such account → `no such account`, stderr, **3**, with no password check performed.
+   (FR-022)
+2. Current password incorrect → `invalid credentials`, stderr, **1**, stored password
+   unchanged, `failed_logins` **not** advanced. (FR-020)
+3. New password under 12 characters → `password too short`, stderr, **2**, stored password
+   unchanged. (FR-021)
+4. Otherwise → replace the password material (new salt, current default iterations), leave
+   `locked` and the tally untouched, exit **0**, no output. The new password is then accepted
+   for login and the previous one rejected. (FR-019)
+
+The current password is verified **before** the new password's length is checked: a request
+with both a wrong current password and a short new password exits **1**. (FR-021)
+
+### `delete USERNAME`
+
+Reads one line: the current password.
+
+1. No such account → `no such account`, stderr, **3**. (FR-025)
+2. Password incorrect → `invalid credentials`, stderr, **1**, account retained unchanged and
+   still accepting its correct password, tally **not** advanced. (FR-024)
+3. Otherwise → remove the record, exit **0**, no output. Every other account is untouched, and
+   the username is free to register again and behaves as an unknown account until it is.
+   (FR-023)
+
+---
+
+## Store-related behaviour
+
+| Condition | Behaviour | Requirement |
+|---|---|---|
+| Store file does not exist | Treated as an empty store. `register` creates it; `login` → `invalid credentials` / 1; `unlock`, `passwd`, `delete` → `no such account` / 3. | FR-002, edge cases |
+| Path is a directory, parent directory missing, or unreadable/unwritable | `store unusable: <cause>`, stderr, **5** | FR-029 |
+| Content is not UTF-8, not JSON, lacks the format marker, or has a record missing a required field | `store unusable: <cause>`, stderr, **5** — never treated as empty | FR-029, FR-029a |
+| Any successful write | Whole file rewritten atomically (temp file in the same directory → flush → fsync → rename), mode `0o600`, never widened | FR-029b, FR-029c |
+| Any failing command | Store left exactly as it was, except the `login` wrong-password tally increment | FR-028 |
+
+See [store-file.md](./store-file.md) for the on-disk format.
+
+---
+
+## Performance contract
+
+- One command completes within **1 second** for a store of up to 1,000 accounts. (SC-009)
+- Exactly one password derivation per command, except `passwd` (two) and `unlock` (none). A
+  command's cost does not otherwise grow with the number of accounts beyond reading and
+  rewriting the file. (SC-009)
+
+---
+
+## Internal entry point (not a user-facing interface)
+
+```python
+accounts.cli.main(argv: list[str], stdin: TextIO, stdout: TextIO, stderr: TextIO) -> int
+```
+
+Returns the exit status instead of raising `SystemExit`, and touches no `sys.*` global.
+`accounts/__main__.py` is the only module that reads `sys.argv`, wraps `sys.stdin.buffer` as
+UTF-8, and calls `sys.exit(...)`.
+
+This exists so the suite can exercise the CLI in-process within the SC-009 suite budget
+(see D-008 in [../research.md](../research.md)). It does not change, extend or duplicate the
+command-line contract above: `python -m accounts` remains the documented interface, and is
+itself asserted end to end through real subprocesses.
